1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,228
You wanted to be a shield-maiden,

2
00:00:02,228 --> 00:00:04,761
like the women in the old stories.

3
00:00:04,761 --> 00:00:06,052
A painted shield,

4
00:00:06,052 --> 00:00:09,694
a fast ship, your braids flying in the wind.

5
00:00:09,694 --> 00:00:11,221
Instead, it is barely light,

6
00:00:11,221 --> 00:00:12,485
you are nine years old,

7
00:00:12,485 --> 00:00:14,801
your feet are soaked, and you are chasing a

8
00:00:14,801 --> 00:00:17,133
sheep through a hole in the fence.

9
00:00:17,133 --> 00:00:20,153
The sheep has escaped again.

10
00:00:20,153 --> 00:00:21,461
Somebody has to catch it.

11
00:00:21,832 --> 00:00:23,300
Since last spring, that somebody is you.

12
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,003
Imagine you are Thora,

13
00:00:25,003 --> 00:00:27,521
the daughter of Eirik and Aslaug,

14
00:00:27,521 --> 00:00:30,733
born in our chapter on Viking women.

15
00:00:30,733 --> 00:00:31,780
Like your parents,

16
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:33,325
you are an imagined person,

17
00:00:33,325 --> 00:00:36,799
built from what archaeology, laws and written sources tell us,

18
00:00:36,799 --> 00:00:38,769
not a real girl from history.

19
00:00:38,769 --> 00:00:41,079
You have your mother's chestnut hair,

20
00:00:41,079 --> 00:00:43,572
in two long braids that never stay tidy,

21
00:00:43,572 --> 00:00:45,091
a pale yellow wool dress

22
00:00:45,091 --> 00:00:47,402
with a brown belt, and a single green

23
00:00:47,402 --> 00:00:50,043
glass bead on a cord around your neck.

24
00:00:50,378 --> 00:00:50,887
Your uncle Asmund

25
00:00:50,887 --> 00:00:52,049
brought it back from the east.

26
00:00:52,484 --> 00:00:56,172
You have never taken it off.

27
00:00:56,172 --> 00:00:58,720
Popular culture likes Viking children

28
00:00:58,720 --> 00:00:59,927
as tiny warriors,

29
00:00:59,927 --> 00:01:03,999
waving wooden swords and growing up to conquer the world.

30
00:01:03,999 --> 00:01:04,943
There were wooden swords.

31
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,776
But most of a Viking childhood looked very different:

32
00:01:09,776 --> 00:01:10,359
short,

33
00:01:10,359 --> 00:01:15,251
busy, and, by our standards, frighteningly dangerous.

34
00:01:15,251 --> 00:01:15,883
Your parents' world,

35
00:01:15,883 --> 00:01:17,027
it turns out, starts handing you jobs

36
00:01:17,027 --> 00:01:18,275
almost as soon as you can walk.

37
00:01:18,618 --> 00:01:20,453
And it expects you to be grown

38
00:01:20,453 --> 00:01:22,640
up long before you feel ready.

39
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:24,284
And at the end of this story,

40
00:01:24,284 --> 00:01:27,025
we will look at a tiny stone animal from Iceland,

41
00:01:27,025 --> 00:01:28,504
made around your lifetime,

42
00:01:28,504 --> 00:01:31,144
that archaeologists still cannot agree on.

43
00:01:31,472 --> 00:01:34,129
Is it a pig?

44
00:01:34,595 --> 00:01:35,586
A bear?

45
00:01:35,586 --> 00:01:36,663
A dog?

46
00:01:36,663 --> 00:01:37,860
Hold that thought.

47
00:01:38,307 --> 00:01:38,902
Your first test came before

48
00:01:38,902 --> 00:01:39,984
you could remember anything.

49
00:01:39,984 --> 00:01:41,328
You passed it simply by

50
00:01:41,328 --> 00:01:43,319
being born and being accepted.

51
00:01:43,724 --> 00:01:45,638
The history site Hurstwic,

52
00:01:45,638 --> 00:01:49,933
drawing on the sagas and laws, describes the ritual.

53
00:01:49,933 --> 00:01:50,994
The mother accepted a

54
00:01:50,994 --> 00:01:52,389
newborn by nursing it.

55
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:54,264
The father showed acceptance by

56
00:01:54,264 --> 00:01:55,720
taking the baby onto his knee,

57
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:58,369
giving it a name, and sprinkling it with water.

58
00:01:58,369 --> 00:02:00,988
That sprinkling with water was

59
00:02:00,988 --> 00:02:04,451
older than Christianity in the north.

60
00:02:04,850 --> 00:02:05,549
The poem Rígsþula,

61
00:02:05,549 --> 00:02:07,351
which imagines how the classes of society began,

62
00:02:07,351 --> 00:02:09,726
describes newborns being sprinkled with water and named.

63
00:02:10,136 --> 00:02:12,685
Your mother tells the story of your

64
00:02:12,685 --> 00:02:16,437
birth every winter: a long night and a long morning,

65
00:02:16,437 --> 00:02:18,702
a neighbor woman who had helped

66
00:02:18,702 --> 00:02:22,071
at many births, and a cradle by the hearth.

67
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:23,315
Your father,

68
00:02:23,315 --> 00:02:25,921
she likes to say, held you on his knee so

69
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:28,472
carefully that he forgot to breathe.

70
00:02:28,909 --> 00:02:30,010
Not every baby was so lucky.

71
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:31,769
The National Museum of Denmark

72
00:02:31,769 --> 00:02:32,847
states it plainly:

73
00:02:32,847 --> 00:02:35,734
newborn babies were sometimes put out to die.

74
00:02:36,272 --> 00:02:38,065
Hurstwic notes that this was usually

75
00:02:38,065 --> 00:02:39,471
done because of a deformity,

76
00:02:39,471 --> 00:02:42,547
or because the family simply could not feed another child.

77
00:02:42,547 --> 00:02:44,854
When Iceland accepted Christianity,

78
00:02:44,854 --> 00:02:46,777
around the year one thousand,

79
00:02:46,777 --> 00:02:49,789
the compromise reached at the Althing at first

80
00:02:49,789 --> 00:02:52,801
left the old law on exposing infants in place,

81
00:02:52,801 --> 00:02:55,050
as a concession to the pagans.

82
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:56,262
It was banned later,

83
00:02:56,262 --> 00:02:58,772
once the Church was firmly in control.

84
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:01,380
The National Museum of Denmark confirms that,

85
00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:02,600
after Christianity came,

86
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:04,767
the practice was forbidden by law.

87
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:06,816
Even accepted babies

88
00:03:06,816 --> 00:03:09,116
faced terrible odds.

89
00:03:09,116 --> 00:03:11,672
As we saw in our chapter on Viking women,

90
00:03:11,672 --> 00:03:13,254
researchers estimate that

91
00:03:13,254 --> 00:03:15,140
around half of all children in

92
00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:18,319
pre-modern societies never reached adulthood.

93
00:03:18,319 --> 00:03:19,191
The National Museum of

94
00:03:19,191 --> 00:03:19,873
Denmark is blunt:

95
00:03:19,873 --> 00:03:21,976
infant mortality in the Viking period was high.

96
00:03:22,412 --> 00:03:25,434
There was a little brother once.

97
00:03:25,434 --> 00:03:27,769
He lived for one winter.

98
00:03:27,769 --> 00:03:30,496
Your mother does not talk about him.

99
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:33,118
Your father carved his name into a

100
00:03:33,118 --> 00:03:36,243
small piece of wood, and keeps it by the bed.

101
00:03:36,243 --> 00:03:38,241
So, at nine, you are already a survivor.

102
00:03:38,241 --> 00:03:41,253
Your reward is a long list of jobs.

103
00:03:41,643 --> 00:03:42,748
The National Museum of

104
00:03:42,748 --> 00:03:44,334
Denmark notes that children were

105
00:03:44,334 --> 00:03:46,228
put to work from a young age.

106
00:03:46,228 --> 00:03:46,714
Indoors,

107
00:03:46,714 --> 00:03:49,589
they helped tend the fire and prepare food.

108
00:03:49,589 --> 00:03:51,215
Outdoors, they helped with the

109
00:03:51,215 --> 00:03:52,473
animals and the fields,

110
00:03:52,473 --> 00:03:54,946
and gathered firewood, berries and fruit.

111
00:03:55,377 --> 00:03:56,574
Hurstwic agrees:

112
00:03:56,574 --> 00:04:01,225
children were expected to work and contribute to the farm.

113
00:04:01,225 --> 00:04:03,788
So your day begins with the fire.

114
00:04:04,237 --> 00:04:06,344
You blow on last night's embers

115
00:04:06,344 --> 00:04:09,767
until they catch, then fetch water from the stream,

116
00:04:09,767 --> 00:04:10,228
twice,

117
00:04:10,228 --> 00:04:14,577
because the first time you spilled half of it on the dog.

118
00:04:14,577 --> 00:04:15,441
Then come the animals.

119
00:04:15,441 --> 00:04:16,620
Your father has made you the

120
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:18,116
official keeper of the sheep.

121
00:04:18,418 --> 00:04:21,119
This is a great honour.

122
00:04:21,119 --> 00:04:23,176
It is also because nobody else wants

123
00:04:23,176 --> 00:04:25,660
to chase that particular sheep anymore.

124
00:04:26,083 --> 00:04:27,052
By now, you can milk a cow

125
00:04:27,052 --> 00:04:28,519
without being kicked, most days.

126
00:04:28,945 --> 00:04:30,565
You know which hen hides her

127
00:04:30,565 --> 00:04:31,961
eggs under the woodpile,

128
00:04:31,961 --> 00:04:34,307
which goat bites, and exactly how far the

129
00:04:34,307 --> 00:04:36,586
sheep can run before it gets bored.

130
00:04:36,924 --> 00:04:38,658
That is more than most

131
00:04:38,658 --> 00:04:41,224
adults in the valley know.

132
00:04:41,224 --> 00:04:43,925
The farm next door has stood empty since Grim,

133
00:04:43,925 --> 00:04:46,510
the neighbor, sailed away to Iceland.

134
00:04:46,510 --> 00:04:48,285
The famous boundary stone is still there.

135
00:04:48,285 --> 00:04:50,988
Your father visits it now and then.

136
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:53,383
He says he is checking the fence.

137
00:04:53,383 --> 00:04:56,629
You think he misses the arguments.

138
00:04:57,082 --> 00:04:57,853
In late summer,

139
00:04:57,853 --> 00:05:00,602
you pick berries along the hillside, and eat roughly one

140
00:05:00,602 --> 00:05:02,964
for every three that go into the basket.

141
00:05:02,964 --> 00:05:03,820
In autumn,

142
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:07,241
you gather firewood, small sticks at first,

143
00:05:07,241 --> 00:05:10,899
then bigger ones as your arms get stronger.

144
00:05:11,234 --> 00:05:14,315
And always, there is wool.

145
00:05:14,315 --> 00:05:15,956
Your mother hands you a spindle almost as

146
00:05:15,956 --> 00:05:17,558
tall as your arm, and you start to learn

147
00:05:17,558 --> 00:05:19,495
the skill that will fill most of your life.

148
00:05:19,495 --> 00:05:21,601
At first, your thread is lumpy,

149
00:05:21,601 --> 00:05:24,776
thin in one place and thick in the next.

150
00:05:24,776 --> 00:05:26,169
Your mother unwinds it,

151
00:05:26,169 --> 00:05:28,472
without a word, and hands it back.

152
00:05:28,878 --> 00:05:31,396
As we saw in our chapter on Viking women,

153
00:05:31,396 --> 00:05:32,954
researchers estimate that

154
00:05:32,954 --> 00:05:35,052
the cloth for a single ship's sail

155
00:05:35,052 --> 00:05:37,609
took years of spinning and weaving.

156
00:05:37,609 --> 00:05:38,847
Somewhere out there,

157
00:05:38,847 --> 00:05:41,163
a sail is waiting for your thread.

158
00:05:41,163 --> 00:05:43,854
It can wait a little longer.

159
00:05:44,156 --> 00:05:45,450
By evening, you are so tired

160
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:46,967
that you fall asleep on the bench

161
00:05:46,967 --> 00:05:48,631
before the porridge is finished.

162
00:05:48,631 --> 00:05:50,761
Your father carries you to bed.

163
00:05:51,116 --> 00:05:54,061
In the morning, the fire needs blowing,

164
00:05:54,061 --> 00:05:55,974
the water needs fetching,

165
00:05:55,974 --> 00:06:00,715
and the sheep, naturally, has found a new hole in the fence.

166
00:06:00,715 --> 00:06:01,530
What you learned

167
00:06:01,530 --> 00:06:03,344
depended on who you were born to.

168
00:06:03,344 --> 00:06:04,652
The poem Rígsþula

169
00:06:04,652 --> 00:06:07,431
imagines three kinds of childhood.

170
00:06:08,043 --> 00:06:08,975
The thrall's children,

171
00:06:08,975 --> 00:06:10,474
as we saw in our chapter on thralls,

172
00:06:10,474 --> 00:06:11,882
spread dung and dug turf.

173
00:06:11,882 --> 00:06:13,480
The free farmer's son,

174
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:16,120
in the poem, learns to break in oxen,

175
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:20,404
build houses and barns, make carts and follow the plough.

176
00:06:20,743 --> 00:06:22,183
The nobleman's son learns

177
00:06:22,183 --> 00:06:23,569
something else entirely:

178
00:06:23,569 --> 00:06:25,509
to swing the shield, bend the bow,

179
00:06:25,509 --> 00:06:28,885
ride horses, loose the hounds, handle a sword and swim.

180
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:31,990
And the youngest noble son,

181
00:06:31,990 --> 00:06:35,745
the poem says, alone learned runes.

182
00:06:36,428 --> 00:06:37,176
It is a poem about society,

183
00:06:37,176 --> 00:06:38,094
not a school timetable.

184
00:06:38,452 --> 00:06:40,140
But it is very clear about who

185
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:42,082
got to ride and who got to dig.

186
00:06:42,082 --> 00:06:43,839
You are a free farmer's daughter.

187
00:06:43,839 --> 00:06:46,803
The poem gives the farmer's daughters names,

188
00:06:46,803 --> 00:06:47,396
but says

189
00:06:47,396 --> 00:06:49,822
nothing about what they learned.

190
00:06:50,213 --> 00:06:51,700
That tells you something too.

191
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:54,032
Even your toys were training.

192
00:06:54,032 --> 00:06:55,784
The National Museum of Denmark

193
00:06:55,784 --> 00:06:57,535
points out that a spinning top

194
00:06:57,535 --> 00:06:59,773
trained the fingers for spinning.

195
00:06:59,773 --> 00:07:02,427
You assumed it was just for fun.

196
00:07:02,782 --> 00:07:03,642
Your mother did not correct you.

197
00:07:03,642 --> 00:07:05,184
Children did play,

198
00:07:05,184 --> 00:07:09,567
though, and the ground has kept some of their toys.

199
00:07:09,567 --> 00:07:11,213
The National Museum of Denmark

200
00:07:11,213 --> 00:07:13,602
lists wooden toys found from the Viking Age,

201
00:07:13,602 --> 00:07:14,823
including small ships,

202
00:07:14,823 --> 00:07:16,909
swords, dolls and animal figures.

203
00:07:16,909 --> 00:07:18,686
In Dublin, then a Norse town,

204
00:07:18,686 --> 00:07:21,706
archaeologists from the National Museum of Ireland

205
00:07:21,706 --> 00:07:23,661
found a little wooden boat about

206
00:07:23,661 --> 00:07:27,248
thirty-seven centimetres long on Winetavern Street.

207
00:07:27,248 --> 00:07:30,825
It has been interpreted as a child's toy.

208
00:07:30,825 --> 00:07:31,439
Somebody,

209
00:07:31,439 --> 00:07:34,875
about a thousand years ago, sailed it in a puddle.

210
00:07:34,875 --> 00:07:36,682
The museum's Viking galleries in

211
00:07:36,682 --> 00:07:38,598
Dublin also display other toys and

212
00:07:38,598 --> 00:07:41,086
board games from the city's excavations.

213
00:07:41,539 --> 00:07:44,283
Your own boat was carved by your father,

214
00:07:44,283 --> 00:07:47,361
one winter evening, from a piece of pine.

215
00:07:47,771 --> 00:07:50,271
It has a mast, a tiny steering oar,

216
00:07:50,271 --> 00:07:53,519
and, at your insistence, a sheep on board.

217
00:07:54,188 --> 00:07:55,136
Your father said Viking

218
00:07:55,136 --> 00:07:56,479
ships did not carry sheep.

219
00:07:56,867 --> 00:07:58,014
You pointed out that his did,

220
00:07:58,014 --> 00:08:00,306
all the time, when they sailed to new lands.

221
00:08:00,306 --> 00:08:01,833
He had no answer to that.

222
00:08:02,204 --> 00:08:04,919
Winter has its own games.

223
00:08:05,621 --> 00:08:06,511
When the lake freezes,

224
00:08:06,511 --> 00:08:08,368
you strap on skates made of bone.

225
00:08:08,368 --> 00:08:11,027
The Swedish History Museum describes them:

226
00:08:11,027 --> 00:08:14,243
skates cut from the foot bones of horses or cattle,

227
00:08:14,243 --> 00:08:16,099
flat and polished underneath,

228
00:08:16,099 --> 00:08:18,968
sometimes rubbed with pig fat to go faster.

229
00:08:18,968 --> 00:08:21,091
The museum's own pair was found at Birka,

230
00:08:21,091 --> 00:08:22,354
the Swedish trading town

231
00:08:22,354 --> 00:08:24,550
where your uncle Asmund used to work.

232
00:08:24,974 --> 00:08:28,228
You did not lift your feet.

233
00:08:28,691 --> 00:08:31,430
You pushed yourself along with a pole.

234
00:08:31,908 --> 00:08:33,320
Hurstwic adds that the poles were

235
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:34,608
often tipped with iron spikes,

236
00:08:34,608 --> 00:08:36,228
and that skating was probably done for

237
00:08:36,228 --> 00:08:38,344
pleasure as well as for getting around.

238
00:08:38,344 --> 00:08:40,954
Your father says skating is for

239
00:08:40,954 --> 00:08:43,935
getting across the lake quickly.

240
00:08:43,935 --> 00:08:44,943
Then he borrows your skates

241
00:08:44,943 --> 00:08:46,442
when he thinks no one is looking.

242
00:08:46,828 --> 00:08:49,382
And then there are the board games.

243
00:08:49,382 --> 00:08:51,710
The adults play them by the fire,

244
00:08:51,710 --> 00:08:54,655
moving little pieces across carved boards,

245
00:08:54,655 --> 00:08:57,052
and you watch until you understand

246
00:08:57,052 --> 00:09:00,058
the rules better than your father does.

247
00:09:00,436 --> 00:09:02,039
The National Museum of Denmark notes

248
00:09:02,039 --> 00:09:03,906
that adults played games together too.

249
00:09:03,906 --> 00:09:05,973
Sometimes, they even let you win.

250
00:09:05,973 --> 00:09:09,169
At Fjörður, a Viking Age farm in eastern Iceland,

251
00:09:09,169 --> 00:09:11,151
archaeologists have even found

252
00:09:11,151 --> 00:09:14,036
a board game carved from volcanic stone.

253
00:09:14,416 --> 00:09:16,075
Remember that site.

254
00:09:16,075 --> 00:09:16,640
We will be back there

255
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:17,688
at the end of this story.

256
00:09:17,688 --> 00:09:18,969
When you are ten,

257
00:09:18,969 --> 00:09:21,157
a boy arrives at the farm.

258
00:09:21,493 --> 00:09:22,309
He is seven,

259
00:09:22,309 --> 00:09:25,072
small, very serious, and he has a cloak far

260
00:09:25,072 --> 00:09:27,414
too fine for a farm like yours.

261
00:09:27,414 --> 00:09:30,334
His name is Orm, and he is Hallvard's grandson,

262
00:09:30,334 --> 00:09:32,785
from the rich farm down the valley.

263
00:09:33,154 --> 00:09:34,852
He has come to be fostered.

264
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:37,398
He will live with your family,

265
00:09:37,398 --> 00:09:38,708
eat at your table,

266
00:09:38,708 --> 00:09:41,727
and learn from your parents, for years.

267
00:09:42,344 --> 00:09:42,669
Colm,

268
00:09:42,669 --> 00:09:46,289
Hallvard's thrall, carries Orm's wooden chest up the hill.

269
00:09:46,289 --> 00:09:48,283
He sets it down by the door,

270
00:09:48,283 --> 00:09:49,728
nods to your mother,

271
00:09:49,728 --> 00:09:53,010
and looks at your father for a long moment.

272
00:09:53,416 --> 00:09:55,315
Your father looks at the fence.

273
00:09:55,315 --> 00:09:56,003
Nobody explains it to you.

274
00:09:56,003 --> 00:09:58,408
You decide to ask Orm later.

275
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:01,340
Orm does not know either.

276
00:10:01,886 --> 00:10:03,474
Fostering was common in the Norse world.

277
00:10:03,474 --> 00:10:05,659
Hurstwic describes it not as adoption,

278
00:10:05,659 --> 00:10:07,059
but as a legal agreement

279
00:10:07,059 --> 00:10:09,474
and an alliance between families.

280
00:10:09,474 --> 00:10:11,313
Usually, a family of lower status

281
00:10:11,313 --> 00:10:13,865
fostered the child of a more prominent one.

282
00:10:13,865 --> 00:10:15,798
The rich family gained loyal friends.

283
00:10:16,136 --> 00:10:16,858
The foster family

284
00:10:16,858 --> 00:10:18,321
gained a powerful connection.

285
00:10:18,775 --> 00:10:20,897
Children also went to

286
00:10:20,897 --> 00:10:23,548
other homes to learn.

287
00:10:23,548 --> 00:10:25,374
The historian Ryder Patzuk-Russell,

288
00:10:25,374 --> 00:10:27,655
writing about education in medieval Iceland,

289
00:10:27,655 --> 00:10:29,278
notes that one way to educate a

290
00:10:29,278 --> 00:10:31,104
child was simply to send them to be

291
00:10:31,104 --> 00:10:32,574
raised in another household,

292
00:10:32,574 --> 00:10:35,377
especially one that could teach important skills.

293
00:10:35,854 --> 00:10:39,124
The sagas take these ties seriously.

294
00:10:39,124 --> 00:10:40,644
Hurstwic notes that the bond

295
00:10:40,644 --> 00:10:42,424
between foster relations could be

296
00:10:42,424 --> 00:10:44,595
as strong as blood, or stronger.

297
00:10:44,595 --> 00:10:48,197
Not every fostering was sentimental.

298
00:10:48,197 --> 00:10:50,255
Hurstwic retells a saga in which

299
00:10:50,255 --> 00:10:52,250
a girl named Ketilríðr was sent

300
00:10:52,250 --> 00:10:54,495
away to be fostered because her own

301
00:10:54,495 --> 00:10:57,251
mother refused to teach her crafts.

302
00:10:57,251 --> 00:10:58,231
Families could be very

303
00:10:58,231 --> 00:11:00,016
practical about these arrangements.

304
00:11:00,331 --> 00:11:01,411
At first, Orm cries every night.

305
00:11:01,411 --> 00:11:04,565
You decide, as the eldest child of the house,

306
00:11:04,565 --> 00:11:07,296
that this is your problem to solve.

307
00:11:07,882 --> 00:11:09,784
You teach him to skate,

308
00:11:09,784 --> 00:11:13,448
to blow on embers, and to catch the sheep.

309
00:11:13,989 --> 00:11:15,501
The first time he tries the bone skates,

310
00:11:15,501 --> 00:11:16,313
he falls over so many

311
00:11:16,313 --> 00:11:18,116
times that the dog stops watching.

312
00:11:18,116 --> 00:11:20,057
By spring, he follows you everywhere.

313
00:11:20,375 --> 00:11:21,183
By summer,

314
00:11:21,183 --> 00:11:24,229
he calls you his sister, and means it.

315
00:11:24,229 --> 00:11:25,567
There were no schools on your farm.

316
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:28,219
Not one.

317
00:11:28,219 --> 00:11:29,880
You learned by watching,

318
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:32,073
copying and getting things wrong

319
00:11:32,073 --> 00:11:34,682
in front of adults who sighed.

320
00:11:34,682 --> 00:11:36,239
Your mother teaches you to spin,

321
00:11:36,239 --> 00:11:39,056
to weave, to milk and to plan the winter stores.

322
00:11:39,056 --> 00:11:42,132
Your father teaches Orm to mend fences,

323
00:11:42,132 --> 00:11:45,504
handle a boat and judge the weather.

324
00:11:45,504 --> 00:11:46,077
Sometimes he teaches you too,

325
00:11:46,077 --> 00:11:46,993
when your mother is not looking.

326
00:11:47,523 --> 00:11:48,457
In the sagas,

327
00:11:48,457 --> 00:11:52,212
foster parents could teach far more than farm work.

328
00:11:52,212 --> 00:11:55,071
Patzuk-Russell points to the saga of Njáll,

329
00:11:55,071 --> 00:11:56,176
a famous lawyer,

330
00:11:56,176 --> 00:11:59,376
who fostered children and taught them law.

331
00:11:59,376 --> 00:12:00,660
Your father knows a great

332
00:12:00,660 --> 00:12:02,383
deal about one boundary stone.

333
00:12:02,383 --> 00:12:03,999
That will have to do.

334
00:12:03,999 --> 00:12:04,940
Another saga,

335
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:08,770
Patzuk-Russell notes, tells of a girl named Ólof who was

336
00:12:08,770 --> 00:12:12,253
carefully trained in fine needlework in Norway.

337
00:12:12,633 --> 00:12:14,127
Your mother hears this,

338
00:12:14,127 --> 00:12:17,069
looks at your lumpy thread, and sighs.

339
00:12:17,069 --> 00:12:18,006
The lessons that matter

340
00:12:18,006 --> 00:12:19,568
most are never called lessons.

341
00:12:19,568 --> 00:12:22,209
You learn which neighbor can be trusted,

342
00:12:22,209 --> 00:12:24,593
which one owes your family a favour,

343
00:12:24,593 --> 00:12:27,860
and how to stay quiet when men are bargaining.

344
00:12:28,256 --> 00:12:29,310
You learn that a good winter

345
00:12:29,310 --> 00:12:31,008
store is worth more than a good story.

346
00:12:31,462 --> 00:12:33,790
And you learn that the sheep always,

347
00:12:33,790 --> 00:12:36,757
always finds the weak spot in the fence.

348
00:12:36,757 --> 00:12:38,718
The National Museum of Denmark

349
00:12:38,718 --> 00:12:40,742
notes that play, for the Norse,

350
00:12:40,742 --> 00:12:44,092
also meant sport, exercise, music and weapons.

351
00:12:44,092 --> 00:12:45,360
So Orm gets a wooden

352
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:47,310
sword for his eighth winter.

353
00:12:47,310 --> 00:12:50,254
You get one too, after a long argument.

354
00:12:50,824 --> 00:12:54,452
The sheep, wisely, keeps its distance.

355
00:12:54,985 --> 00:12:55,678
Childhood did not last long.

356
00:12:55,678 --> 00:12:57,287
The National Museum of Denmark

357
00:12:57,287 --> 00:12:58,793
notes that children probably

358
00:12:58,793 --> 00:13:01,089
became adults much earlier than today.

359
00:13:01,682 --> 00:13:02,562
In Iceland,

360
00:13:02,562 --> 00:13:05,771
males were legally adults at sixteen.

361
00:13:05,771 --> 00:13:07,363
Hurstwic adds that by sixteen,

362
00:13:07,363 --> 00:13:08,750
a boy was expected to take

363
00:13:08,750 --> 00:13:10,724
on all the roles of a grown man.

364
00:13:11,252 --> 00:13:13,145
For girls, the line was different.

365
00:13:13,145 --> 00:13:14,554
According to the museum,

366
00:13:14,554 --> 00:13:16,358
girls often married very young,

367
00:13:16,358 --> 00:13:19,766
and were regarded as adults once they were married.

368
00:13:19,766 --> 00:13:22,143
Hurstwic notes that virtually all

369
00:13:22,143 --> 00:13:25,260
women were married by the age of twenty.

370
00:13:25,260 --> 00:13:26,178
Remember Gudrun,

371
00:13:26,178 --> 00:13:28,014
from our chapter on Viking women,

372
00:13:28,014 --> 00:13:31,325
promised in marriage at fifteen, without being asked.

373
00:13:31,325 --> 00:13:32,504
In five or six years,

374
00:13:32,504 --> 00:13:34,595
the families in your valley will start

375
00:13:34,595 --> 00:13:36,692
looking at you and counting cows.

376
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:39,510
When your uncle Asmund visits,

377
00:13:39,510 --> 00:13:42,336
he tells stories about rapids called Do

378
00:13:42,336 --> 00:13:45,770
not sleep and a city with a single gate.

379
00:13:45,770 --> 00:13:48,333
You announce that you will go east too.

380
00:13:48,895 --> 00:13:49,905
Your mother says no.

381
00:13:49,905 --> 00:13:50,510
Your father says no.

382
00:13:50,510 --> 00:13:51,685
Asmund says no,

383
00:13:51,685 --> 00:13:55,531
and then winks at you when they are not looking.

384
00:13:56,036 --> 00:13:56,904
You do not want to

385
00:13:56,904 --> 00:13:58,091
think about marriage yet.

386
00:13:58,091 --> 00:14:00,442
Your mother does not want you to either.

387
00:14:00,442 --> 00:14:02,659
But you notice that she has started

388
00:14:02,659 --> 00:14:04,815
teaching you how to keep the keys,

389
00:14:04,815 --> 00:14:07,402
how to count the stores, and how to argue

390
00:14:07,402 --> 00:14:10,255
with a merchant about the price of cloth.

391
00:14:10,732 --> 00:14:11,319
You are ten.

392
00:14:11,319 --> 00:14:13,016
You are already being

393
00:14:13,016 --> 00:14:15,130
prepared to run a farm.

394
00:14:15,552 --> 00:14:15,860
Orm,

395
00:14:15,860 --> 00:14:19,592
meanwhile, is being prepared to run a much bigger one.

396
00:14:19,592 --> 00:14:21,156
One day, he will go back down

397
00:14:21,156 --> 00:14:22,773
the valley to Hallvard's hall,

398
00:14:22,773 --> 00:14:24,756
with its silver and its thralls.

399
00:14:25,307 --> 00:14:28,214
You wonder what kind of master he will be.

400
00:14:28,811 --> 00:14:30,031
You hope your family's table

401
00:14:30,031 --> 00:14:31,524
will have taught him something.

402
00:14:31,524 --> 00:14:32,979
So why was being a Viking

403
00:14:32,979 --> 00:14:35,030
child worse than you think?

404
00:14:35,030 --> 00:14:37,752
Because you were lucky just to be accepted,

405
00:14:37,752 --> 00:14:39,361
luckier still to survive,

406
00:14:39,361 --> 00:14:40,660
and then put to work

407
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:43,303
before you could carry a full bucket.

408
00:14:43,303 --> 00:14:44,554
Childhood ended at sixteen,

409
00:14:44,554 --> 00:14:47,389
or with a wedding, and nobody asked if you were ready.

410
00:14:47,389 --> 00:14:49,531
And yet, there were toy boats with

411
00:14:49,531 --> 00:14:52,347
sheep on board, bone skates on a frozen lake,

412
00:14:52,347 --> 00:14:53,877
board games by the fire,

413
00:14:53,877 --> 00:14:56,525
and foster brothers who became family.

414
00:14:57,133 --> 00:14:58,810
Viking childhood was hard,

415
00:14:58,810 --> 00:15:01,091
but it was not without joy.

416
00:15:01,091 --> 00:15:02,704
And it is almost invisible.

417
00:15:03,293 --> 00:15:05,946
As we saw in our chapter on Viking women,

418
00:15:05,946 --> 00:15:07,463
babies and children are

419
00:15:07,463 --> 00:15:10,055
under-represented in Viking burials.

420
00:15:10,397 --> 00:15:11,359
Their toys were made

421
00:15:11,359 --> 00:15:13,028
of wood and rotted away.

422
00:15:13,028 --> 00:15:15,313
Their names were rarely carved in stone.

423
00:15:15,313 --> 00:15:19,102
Which brings us to that little animal.

424
00:15:19,102 --> 00:15:20,338
In twenty twenty-four,

425
00:15:20,338 --> 00:15:23,188
archaeologists digging a Viking Age farm at Fjörður,

426
00:15:23,188 --> 00:15:25,177
in Seyðisfjörður in eastern Iceland,

427
00:15:25,177 --> 00:15:28,028
found a tiny figure carved from volcanic stone.

428
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:30,494
It is about five centimetres long,

429
00:15:30,494 --> 00:15:31,945
and dates to between nine

430
00:15:31,945 --> 00:15:34,209
hundred and forty and one thousand.

431
00:15:34,792 --> 00:15:36,032
It may have been a toy.

432
00:15:36,423 --> 00:15:38,849
And nobody can agree what it is.

433
00:15:38,849 --> 00:15:40,379
Some of the team see a pig.

434
00:15:40,728 --> 00:15:41,870
Others see a bear.

435
00:15:42,247 --> 00:15:43,960
Some people suggest a dog.

436
00:15:44,431 --> 00:15:45,787
The excavation manager,

437
00:15:45,787 --> 00:15:49,516
Ragnheiður Traustadóttir, who has owned an Icelandic dog herself,

438
00:15:49,516 --> 00:15:51,549
said that when you hold the object,

439
00:15:51,549 --> 00:15:53,452
it does not feel like a dog.

440
00:15:53,452 --> 00:15:55,412
She called the find amazing,

441
00:15:55,412 --> 00:15:59,265
because children are not very visible in the Viking Age,

442
00:15:59,265 --> 00:16:01,612
and their toys seldom survive.

443
00:16:02,292 --> 00:16:04,450
Somewhere, a thousand years ago,

444
00:16:04,450 --> 00:16:07,780
a child knew exactly what that animal was.

445
00:16:07,780 --> 00:16:08,680
That child probably named it, too.

446
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:09,689
Perhaps one day,

447
00:16:09,689 --> 00:16:13,368
someone will dig up a little pine boat with a sheep on board,

448
00:16:13,368 --> 00:16:15,558
and argue about that as well.

449
00:16:15,558 --> 00:16:16,255
Next spring,

450
00:16:16,255 --> 00:16:18,559
Bjorn will talk about another voyage.

451
00:16:18,915 --> 00:16:21,553
Your father will pretend not to listen,

452
00:16:21,553 --> 00:16:23,389
and Orm will beg to go.

453
00:16:23,389 --> 00:16:25,225
You will be at the fence,

454
00:16:25,225 --> 00:16:29,371
with your spindle in one hand and a rope in the other.

455
00:16:29,706 --> 00:16:31,828
Because somewhere behind the house,

456
00:16:31,828 --> 00:16:33,537
the sheep has escaped again,

457
00:16:33,537 --> 00:16:35,641
and this time, it is your job.

458
00:16:36,029 --> 00:16:37,329
This is Viking Chapters,

459
00:16:37,329 --> 00:16:39,078
the lives behind the legends.

460
00:16:39,078 --> 00:16:41,048
If you would like to see another

461
00:16:41,048 --> 00:16:42,539
life behind the legends,

462
00:16:42,539 --> 00:16:45,820
subscribe, and we will meet in the next chapter.
